WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.813
How this scales to real networks.

2
00:00:02.093 --> 00:00:12.867
The lecture’s summary at 02:14:03 makes the point that everything you just did is what large networks do, with three differences of scale rather than kind.

3
00:00:13.147 --> 00:00:19.888
Values become tensors, so one node is a whole matrix multiply with a hand-written, vectorized backward.

4
00:00:20.168 --> 00:00:27.720
The dataset is too large to use in every step, so each step estimates the gradient from a random minibatch (lesson 4).

5
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.333
And the loss is cross-entropy over a vocabulary rather than squared error on one output.

6
00:00:33.613 --> 00:00:38.328
This is a useful lens for reading about large models as an applied engineer.

7
00:00:38.608 --> 00:00:48.208
When a model card describes pretraining, fine-tuning, or preference tuning, each one is “pick a loss, compute its gradient on batches of data, step the parameters.

8
00:00:48.488 --> 00:00:51.539
” What changes between them is the data and the loss.

9
00:00:51.819 --> 00:00:58.304
If you took Studio 5, you saw supervised fine-tuning and LoRA from the outside; this is the inside.
